// HumanityOS Food, Spoilage, Nutrition, and Cooking System
// Nutrition profiles, preservation methods, cooking methods, meal quality and
// temperature zones. Loaded by src/systems/food.rs (disk first, embedded copy
// as the fallback).
//
// WHICH ITEM USES WHICH PROFILE is decided by data/food/item_profiles.ron, not
// by item-id prefixes. An item that is not listed there is not food: it cannot
// be eaten or drunk and it never spoils.
//
// Nutrition profiles carry macro and micronutrients per 100 g.
//   calories_per_100g, protein_g, carbs_g, fat_g, fiber_g: per 100 g.
//   minerals: milligrams per 100 g.
//   vitamins: fraction of the US FDA Daily Value per 100 g (1.0 = 100% DV).
//     For the rows added 2026-09-25 "B" is the mean %DV of six B vitamins
//     (thiamin, riboflavin, niacin, B6, folate, B12). The first twelve rows
//     predate that rule and are rougher category estimates.
//   spoilage_rate_hours: how long the food stays safe under the storage it
//     normally gets (refrigerator for perishables, pantry for shelf-stable
//     food). For the rows added 2026-09-25 this is the LOWER bound of the USDA
//     FSIS FoodKeeper range for that food, which is also what the older rows
//     match (raw meat 24 h = ground meat 1-2 days, eggs 504 h = 3-5 weeks).
//     It is NOT a room-temperature figure: the game has no cold storage yet,
//     so this is the one number that stands for "kept properly".
//   raw_consumption_risk: chance (0..1) of food poisoning when eaten as-is.
//     These are game-scale judgements, not measured probabilities; the notes
//     on each row say what real hazard they stand for.
//   category: protein, produce, staple, preserved, beverage, meal, baked, fat,
//     sweetener. food.rs reads it for hydration: a "beverage" hydrates like a
//     drink, "produce" hydrates a little, the rest barely.
//
// Sources for the rows added 2026-09-25, all downloaded 2026-09-25:
//   * USDA FoodData Central, SR Legacy release (April 2018), cited by NDB number.
//   * USDA FoodData Central, FNDDS 2021-2023 (released 2024-10-31), cited by
//     FNDDS food code, for mixed dishes SR Legacy does not carry.
//   * USDA FSIS FoodKeeper storage data, read from the 2019 copy published in
//     github.com/jelera/food-shelflife-db (lib/seeds/ingredients.csv); the
//     fsis.usda.gov download refused an automated request that day.
//   * Meal, Ready-to-Eat facts from en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meal,_Ready-to-Eat
//     (1,200 to 1,250 kcal a menu, 510 to 740 g packed, shelf life three and a
//     half years at 27 C) and the DLA figure of 1,250 kcal at 13% protein,
//     36% fat, 51% carbohydrate as quoted by mreinfo.com.
//
// Preservation methods multiply base shelf life. Multiple methods do NOT stack.
// Cooking methods modify nutrition, taste, preservation, and require cooking skill.
// Meal quality is determined by ingredients, cooking method, and cook skill level.
// (Preservation, cooking, meal quality and temperature zones are loaded but no
// code acts on them yet.)
//
// Status effect IDs reference status_effects.csv.

(
